    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "that makes sense. can u go a bit deeper into the biology part? like how does the breath actually trigger the nervous system to change? is there a specific nerve involved?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The mechanism is largely tied to the **vagus nerve**, which is the main highway of the parasympathetic nervous system—your body's \"rest and digest\" system. When you breathe slowly and deeply, especially with that controlled rhythm, you stimulate the vagus nerve. This stimulation triggers a biological cascade that slows down your heart rate and promotes relaxation. Think of it like gently persuading a highly agitated nervous system to settle down, rather than forcing it.",
      "source": "target",
